<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-s07e04" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Episode 4: Solving Equations Requiring Distribution</title>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Episode 4 Learning Goals</title>
    <p>
      Solve equations that require distribution
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <p>
    You may need to use distribution to solve certain equations.
  </p>
  <example>
    <p>
      Solve:
      <md>
        2(x-1)=3x+4
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution: Start by distributing the 2 on the left side of the equation:
      <md>
        2x-2=3x+4
      </md>
      Now we can solve the equation using the technique we learned in Episode 2. We can subtract <m>2x</m> from both sides:
      <md>
        2x-2 -2x = 3x+4 -2x
      </md>
      <md>
        -2=x+4
      </md>
      And then we can subtract 4 from both sides:
      <md>
        -2-4 = x + 4 - 4
      </md>
      <md>
        -6 = x
      </md>
    </p>
  </example>
  <p>
    <alert>General Process for Solving Equations</alert>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          If necessary, distribute. 
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Add or subtract terms to cancel out all the variables on one side of the equation.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Clean up the equation by combining like terms.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Add or subtract terms to cancel out all the numbers on the other side of the equation.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Clean up the equation by combining like terms.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          If necessary, finish isolating the variable using multiplication / division.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </p>
  <example>
    <p>
      Solve the equation:
      <md>
        \frac{1}{2}(x-4) = \frac{1}{3}(x+4)
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution: Start by distributing:
      <md>
        \frac{1}{2}x-\frac{1}{2}\times 4 = \frac{1}{3}x + \frac{1}{3}\times 4
      </md>
      Finish the multiplications <m>\left(\frac{1}{2}\times 4\right)</m> and <m>\left(\frac{1}{3}\times 4\right)</m>
      <md>
        \frac{1}{2}x-2=\frac{1}{3}x+\frac{4}{3}
      </md>
      Subtract <m>\frac{1}{3}x</m> from both sides to cancel the variables on the right:
      <md>
        \frac{1}{2}x-2-\frac{1}{3}x = \frac{1}{3}x+\frac{4}{3}-\frac{1}{3}x
      </md>
      <md>
        \frac{1}{2}x-2 - \frac{1}{3}x = \frac{4}{3}
      </md>
      Combine like terms by subtracting the fractions <m>\left(\frac{1}{2}-\frac{1}{3}\right)</m>:
      <md>
        \frac{1}{6}x-2=\frac{4}{3}
      </md>
      Add 2 to both sides to cancel the number on the left:
      <md>
        \frac{1}{6}x-2+2 = \frac{4}{3}+2
      </md>
      <md>
        \frac{1}{6}x = \frac{4}{3} + 2
      </md>
      Combine like terms by adding the fractions <m>\left(\frac{4}{3}+2\right)</m>:
      <md>
        \frac{1}{6}x = \frac{10}{3}
      </md>
      Finally, isolate <m>x</m> by dividing by 1/6:
      <md>
        \frac{1}{6}x \div\frac{1}{6} = \frac{10}{3}\div \frac{1}{6}
      </md>
    <md>
      x = \frac{10}{3}\times 6
    </md>
    Finish the problem by multiplying the fractions <m>\left( \frac{10}{3}\times 6\right)</m>:
    <md>
      x=20
    </md>
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      Solve the equation:
      <md>
        7(x+3)+1=4(x-2)
      </md>
      Solution: Start by distributing:
      <md>
        7x+7\cdot 3+1 = 4x-4\cdot 2
      </md>
      <md>
        7x+21 + 1 = 4x-8
      </md>
      We can clean up the equation by combining like terms:
      <md>
        7x+22=4x-8
      </md>
      Subtract <m>4x</m> from both sides to cancel the variables on the right, then combine like terms again:
      <md>
        7x+22-4x=4x-8-4x
      </md>
      <md>
        3x+22=-8
      </md>
      Subtract 22 from both sides to cancel the numbers on the left, then combine like terms again:
      <md>
        3x+22-22 = -8-22
      </md>
      <md>
        3x=-30
      </md>
      Finish isolating <m>x</m> by dividing both sides by 3:
      <md>
        \frac{3x}{3} = \frac{-30}{3}
      </md>
      <md>
        x=-10
      </md>
    </p>
  </example>
  <worksheet xml:id="ws-s07e04">
    <title>Season 7, Episode 4 Practice Problems</title>
    <p>
      Solve the following equations:
    </p>
    <exercise workspace="2.25in">
      <p><m>3(x-4)=2x+5</m></p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="2.25in">
      <p><m>\frac{1}{2}(m-8)=\frac{3}{4}m+1</m></p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="2.25in">
      <p><m>-2(y+7)=4y-6</m></p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="2.25in">
      <p><m>5(2a-3)+4=3a-11</m></p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="2.25in">
      <p><m>-0.5(p-6)=1.5p+2</m></p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="2.25in">
      <p><m>7-3(t-2)=2t+1</m></p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="2.25in">
      <p><m>\frac{2}{3}(k+9)-4=k</m></p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="2.25in">
      <p><m>1.2(r+5)=0.4r-3</m></p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="2.25in">
      <p><m>-4(2z+1)+6=-2z</m></p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="2.25in">
      <p><m>3(2x-5)=4(x+1)-7</m></p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="2.25in">
      <p><m>\frac{5}{6}(n-12)+2=\frac{1}{3}n</m></p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="2.25in">
      <p><m>-2(y-3)+5=0.5(4y+6)</m></p>
    </exercise>
  </worksheet>
</section> 